    "scientific_physical_explanation": "The 215.8-hour continuous daylight window is physically genuine and directly attributable to lunar orbital mechanics. At Malapert A (80.13\u00b0 S), the site experiences approximately 14 Earth days of daylight and 14 Earth days of night during each 29.53-day synodic lunar cycle. The IM-1 Odysseus landing on February 22, 2024 took place during the middle of the lunar day (Sun elevation ~9.65\u00b0). Over the following 9.0 Earth days (215.8 hours), the Sun steadily tracked counter-clockwise around the sky while slowly descending toward the horizon. At 2024-03-01T23:45:00Z, the Sun's elevation was +2.029\u00b0, clearing the local horizon of 1.996\u00b0 by +0.033\u00b0. 15 minutes later at 2024-03-02T00:00:00Z, the Sun dropped to +1.954\u00b0 while the terrain horizon stood at +2.008\u00b0 (margin -0.054\u00b0), initiating the multi-week lunar night. Thus, the 215.8-hour window is not a calculation artifact; it represents the exact remaining daylight duration from landing until the natural local sunset obstructed by the northwestern crater rim."
  },
